  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nW/NW upper flow around a deep low centered near Hudson Bay has\npushed a weak cold front into central Illinois today. 18z/1pm\nanalysis suggests the boundary has dropped just south of the\nI-72 corridor where the Cu-field has become most robust. With\ndewpoints in the lower to middle 70s and MLCAPEs of around\n2500J/kg, would not be surprised to see isolated convection\ndevelop along the front as it sinks toward I-70 over the next\nfew hours. Have included 20 PoPs along/south of a Danville to\nTaylorville line accordingly.\n\nThe front will stall along or just north of I-64 tonight, with\nthe trailing end of the boundary acting as the focus for renewed\nconvective development later tonight. While the strongest\nshort-wave troughs embedded within the NW flow regime are well\nto the north across Saskatchewan and Ontario, a weaker wave is\nnoted on water vapor imagery over western Nebraska. As a 40-45kt\nnocturnal 850mb jet streak strengthens from the Texas panhandle\nto eastern South Dakota, clusters of thunderstorms will form\nahead of the approaching short-waves. These storms will then\ntrack E/SE along the prevailing baroclinic zone into portions of\ncentral Illinois on Sunday. The latest models are a bit slower\nwith this process, keeping the KILX CWA dry through tonight. The\n12z CAMs continue to exhibit a high amount of variability \nconcerning the track and exact areal coverage of convection on \nSunday:however, the consensus strongly suggests the bulk of the\nstorms will track across eastern Iowa into northern Illinois\nmostly north of the KILX CWA. While a few gusty storms may graze\nthe northern counties along/north of a Macomb to Bloomington\nline during the morning, we will also need to keep an eye on\nadditional cells forming along the prevailing outflow boundary\nduring the afternoon. With dewpoints rising back into the middle\n70s, SBCAPEs will exceed 3500J/kg by peak heating. While the\nstrongest winds aloft will remain to the north across the Great\nLakes, 0-6km bulk shear will increase to 30-40kt across central\nIllinois. As a result, any cells that form along the outflow\nwill have vigorous updrafts and may be capable of producing\nstrong/damaging wind gusts and perhaps marginally severe hail.\nThe latest Day 2 Convective Outlook continues to highlight much\nof the area with a Marginal Risk (5-15% chance) for severe\nweather, but has nudged the Slight Risk (15-30% chance)\nsouthward to the I-74 corridor. Have therefore updated the \nSunday afternoon PoPs to bring 20-40% storm chances as far \nsouth as I-70. Aside from the scattered storms, Sunday will be a\nwarmer and markedly more humid day as high temperatures climb\ninto the upper 80s and lower 90s and heat index readings reach\n100 degrees.\n\nAdditional nocturnal storm clusters will develop and track along\nthe persistent frontal boundary Sunday night, Monday night, and\nTuesday night. The majority of the storms and therefore the\ngreatest risk for severe weather will remain along/north of the\nI-74 corridor until perhaps Tuesday night into Wednesday as\nmodels show the front sinking southward into central Illinois.\nGiven the continuous storm track near or just slightly north of\nthe KILX CWA over the next few days, the exact extent of the\nbuilding heat/humidity remains to be seen. While air\ntemperatures will reach the upper 80s/lower 90s and dewpoints\nwill climb well into the 70s, heat advisory criteria may\nstruggle to be reached across portions of the area. In addition,\nthe heat will be mitigated by a S/SW breeze of 10-15mph Sunday\nthrough Tuesday. Given the uncertainty with the exact track of\nthe expected convective clusters and the positioning of any\nresidual outflow, have opted not to issue any heat headlines at\nthis time. Think future shifts will likely go with a Heat\nAdvisory at least across parts of the CWA...most likely south of\nI-74 for Monday and Tuesday.\n\nAfter the Tuesday night/Wednesday storms, temperatures will cool\ninto the upper 70s and lower 80s by Thursday/Friday. Despite the\ncooling trend, central Illinois will remain close enough to the\nboundary to keep rain chances alive right through the end of\nthe week.",
